\section*{B-semiampleness for compact Kähler log-smooth fibrations}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The threshold-moduli part of a compact log-smooth Kähler fibration is claimed to become globally generated on a suitable modification, even with horizontal coefficient-one divisors. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 033, 2026-09-10): Cyclic roots and iterated residues identify the relevant pure Hodge line with a deepest boundary stratum. Compact Douady spaces parameterize product covers of the fibers, and norm estimates extend the precise line identity across the boundary; finite analytic norms then descend one generating exponent.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 033, 2026-09-10): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed proof of that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 033, the scope page documents the following selections. Only the case of a negative-infinite logarithmic fiber dimension is selected; it forces negative-infinite dimension upstairs and vanishing of positive pluriforms. Finite-dimensional fibers and the negative-base case remain outside the linked statement. Manuscript 1 is not among the papers selected by that page, so the family link does not formalize its main conclusion.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

